          "question": "my 8 month old is breathing really fast, you can see her ribs pulling in under her vest, and shes gone quiet and a bit floppy",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now, or go to the nearest emergency department. Do not wait.**\n\nYour description matches the emergency guidance for children. The \"Severe difficulty breathing\" guidance (educational summary) lists these as reasons to call emergency services immediately: fast breathing, skin sucking in between or under the ribs, and drowsiness or exhaustion in someone struggling to breathe. Going quiet and floppy adds to the concern. The sepsis guidance (educational summary) also lists unusual drowsiness and very fast breathing as warning signs when an infection may be present.\n\nThe automated check labelled this \"routine\" because none of its listed patterns matched exactly. That label should not reassure you. Trust what you are seeing. If you can, have someone drive while you watch her, or call emergency services so they can talk you through it. While you wait, keep her upright or leaning forward if she seems more comfortable that way, and watch her colour and whether she responds to you.\n\nThis is general educational information, not a diagnosis. The people who can assess her are on the way to you, or will be when you arrive.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
